\section{Discussion and Conclusion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Scope and limitations.}
All findings are correlational (confounded by data distribution and optimization; $N = 2$ VLM-to-VLA transitions; Appendix~\ref{sec:claim_evidence_appendix}); we analyze causal-decoder VLAs on LIBERO only (flow-matching~\cite{black2024_pi0} and diffusion~\cite{ghosh2024_octo} architectures may differ); all analyses are open-loop.
Trained $\Delta R^2$ correlates with published success rates ($\rho = 0.583$, $p = 0.10$; Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_discussion_appendix}) but is underpowered ($n = 9$; effect size is large, $|\rho| > 0.5$); tracing collapse onset remains for future work.

\paragraph{Practical implications.}
$\Delta R^2$ provides a cheap diagnostic ($> 0.10$ signals reorganization); preserved VLA-to-VLA exit structure justifies staged pipelines; mid-layer probes outperform exit-layer probes where collapse is pronounced (Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_discussion_appendix}).

\paragraph{Conclusion.}
Across three architectures and four LIBERO suites, VLM-to-VLA fine-tuning produces exit collapse ($\Delta R^2 = 0.15$--$0.45$) while VLA-to-VLA conditions remain flat ($< 0.10$).
All trained VLAs show graded counterfactual sensitivity ($d = 0.45$--$1.52$); L1 instruction attention is inherited from pretrained backbones; NLP controls ($\Delta R^2 = 0.000$) confirm transition-type specificity, providing reusable diagnostics for future VLA evaluation and training pipeline design.
Code and data will be released upon acceptance.

